Below is a step‐by‐step decomposition of the mission description into a goal model for multi‐robot systems. In our design we break down the overall mission into three major sub-goals:
  
• Delivering food (with two alternative methods plus meal tracking)  
• Retrieving dirty dishes (with several alternatives)  
• Opening the inpatient room door (with alternatives for human cooperation)

Each sub-goal is further decomposed into children goals and tasks. We also explain the chosen runtime annotations and relations (AND vs. OR, sequential “;” vs. fallback “FALLBACK(…)”) according to the mission description.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Overall Goal Decomposition

We start with the top-level goal:

**G1: Deliver Food and Retrieve Dishes to Inpatient Room**  
– This is an Achieve goal since the mission is to bring about a world state (food delivered, dishes retrieved, door opened).  
– Its overall runtime annotation is defined by the ordering: the room door must be opened first (sequential), then both food delivery and dish retrieval can be done concurrently (parallel). Hence, we annotate it as:  
  G1.3 ; (G1.1 # G1.2)  
– This reflects that:  
  – G1.3 (door opening) is a prerequisite executed sequentially  
  – G1.1 (food delivery) and G1.2 (dish retrieval) are alternatives that can run in parallel once access is ensured

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Sub-Goal Decompositions

A. Food Delivery (G1.1)  
This goal involves two alternative methods for food delivery, plus a subsequent meal tracking step.  
• G1.1.1: Direct Table Delivery – A Perform goal where the robot uses its special manipulation skills.  
• G1.1.2: Tray Retrieval – A Perform goal that requires cooperation (from the inpatient, companion, nurse, or another robot).  
• G1.1.3: Meal Tracking and Alerting – An Achieve goal ensuring that the robot updates the meal log and alerts if a wrong meal is retrieved.  

Since G1.1.1 and G1.1.2 are alternatives (i.e. one is sufficient) and G1.1.3 must follow the delivery, we use a fallback for the alternatives and a sequential operator for the tracking step. Thus, the runtime annotation for G1.1 is:  
  FALLBACK(G1.1.1, G1.1.2); G1.1.3

B. Dish Retrieval (G1.2)  
This goal has three alternatives:  
• G1.2.1: Unassisted Dish Retrieval – A Perform goal executed by a single robot.  
• G1.2.2: Dish Retrieval with Two Robots – A Perform goal where two robots collaborate.  
• G1.2.3: Dish Retrieval with Human Assistance – A Perform goal where a robot and a human work together.  

With three alternatives, we nest the fallback operator:  
  FALLBACK(G1.2.1, FALLBACK(G1.2.2, G1.2.3))

C. Open Room Door (G1.3)  
This goal provides two alternatives for door opening:  
• G1.3.1: Self Door Opening – A Perform goal where the robot opens the door alone.  
• G1.3.2: Human-Assisted Door Opening – A Perform goal where the door is opened with human help.  

The runtime annotation for G1.3 is:  
  FALLBACK(G1.3.1, G1.3.2)

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Task Decomposition

Each leaf goal (i.e. those with runtime annotation “-”) corresponds to a concrete, operational task. Below is the mapping from goals to tasks:

• For G1.1.1: Direct Table Delivery  
  Task AT1: “Deliver to Table” – executed at the inpatient room (table area) by a single robot with specialized skills.

• For G1.1.2: Tray Retrieval  
  Task AT2: “Retrieve from Tray” – executed at the inpatient room by one robot, with cooperation from humans as needed.

• For G1.1.3: Meal Tracking and Alerting  
  Task AT3: “Track and Alert Meal” – performed at the hospital system (or in the room) by one robot to update logs and issue alerts.

• For G1.2.1: Unassisted Dish Retrieval  
  Task AT4: “Retrieve Dishes Unassisted” – executed in the inpatient room by a single robot.

• For G1.2.2: Dish Retrieval with Two Robots  
  Task AT5: “Retrieve Dishes with Two Robots” – executed in the inpatient room by two robots collaborating.

• For G1.2.3: Dish Retrieval with Human Assistance  
  Task AT6: “Retrieve Dishes with Human Assistance” – executed in the inpatient room by one robot with human support.

• For G1.3.1: Self Door Opening  
  Task AT7: “Open Door Self” – executed at the hospital room entrance by a single robot.

• For G1.3.2: Human-Assisted Door Opening  
  Task AT8: “Open Door with Assistance” – executed at the hospital room entrance by one robot with human help.

